Actually, aimake itself is designed to be portable to a wide range of
operating systems. However, it comes with a configuration file, which
needs to be different for each platform, and so far, I've only written a
configuration file for GNU-style platforms. So although it can support
Windows in theory, it has never been configured to do so.

If you want to have a go at configuring it yourself, the config file is at
the bottom of aimake, after the __DATA__ line. (You'll see TODOs where
there are OS-specific values that are currently hardcoded.) Otherwise, I
guess I'll have a go at it eventually, when I can get access to a Windows
system and an appropriate toolchain.

