> /strawberry/c/bin/../lib/gcc/x86_64-w64-mingw32/4.4.7/../../../;
> /mingw/lib: No such file or directory at ../aimake line 1142
> 
> Yeah, I've got no idea what's going on there.

You've caught an aimake bug, which is the result of poor documentation.
Basically, what happened is that the aimake failures reported in Usenet
earlier were due to multilib. It seems that nowadays, in order to support
multiple architectures, gcc uses a weirdly complex subdirectory structure
to store the libraries it links against; and I was looking for libraries
in just the "usual places" before, rather than the subdirectories that the
libraries are actually in. Trying to work out the actual locations would
need information about how gcc was built; but it must be possible for gcc
itself to know the locations, so I decided simply to ask it. After some
looking through manuals, I found a gcc option that gave me the locations,
but the documentation didn't specify the format, so I had to guess.

What I saw when I actually ran the command was a key-value store with a
list of directory names separated by colons, so I guessed that that was
the format. However, based on the message, it seems instead that it's
using the native operating system's format for the PATH environment
variable (which is of course a list of directory names separated by colons
on Linux; but on Windows, they're separated by semicolons instead). I've
now fixed this issue, I hope, so you may be able to get your Cygwin build
working now, upon downloading the latest version of NetHack 4 from the
usual place. (Although further issues wouldn't surprise me.)

